@@ -0,0 +1,42 @@
+---
+id: dl-002-json-patch-atomic-non-mutating-applypatch-with-strict-rfc-6902-semantics
+type: decision-log
+title: "JSON Patch: atomic, non-mutating applyPatch with strict RFC 6902 semantics"
+status: pending
+format: 1
+---
+
+<!-- decision-log body. `wingfoil memory add` copies this scaffold, setting only `id`, `title` and
+     `status` (and any `--tags` or `--set` value). Fill the required frontmatter fields and replace
+     these placeholder comments yourself; `wingfoil memory submit` then checks the required fields,
+     moves `status` forward and commits the document as you left it, naming in the commit body the
+     content it carries. -->
+
+# Context
+
+Services need to change JSON documents with standard patches. RFC 6902 leaves some cases open, and
+`applyPatch` builds on the strict pointer semantics of dl-001.
+
+# Decision
+
+- The input document and the patch are never mutated: the document is deep-copied first, and values
+  taken from the patch (`add`, `replace`, `copy`) are copied too, so the result shares nothing.
+- The patch is atomic: it is validated whole before anything runs, and any failure throws, so no
+  partial result is ever returned.
+- Invalid patches (not an array, bad operation object, unknown `op`, missing or non-string `path`/`from`,
+  missing `value`) and failed operations throw a plain `Error`, as in dl-001.
+- `add` into an array accepts an index from 0 to length, or `-` to append; into an object it sets the
+  member, replacing any existing one. `-` is accepted only as an `add`/`move`/`copy` target.
+- `remove` and `replace` require the target to exist. `replace` of the root is allowed.
+- Removing the whole document (`remove` with the empty path) throws, since no JSON value would result.
+- `move` is remove then add; moving a location into one of its own children throws, and moving a
+  location onto itself is a no-op.
+- `test` uses JSON equality: object member order is ignored, numbers and strings compare by value,
+  and types must match. A mismatch throws.
+- Members are written as own data properties, so a `__proto__` key stays an ordinary member.
+
+# Consequences
+
+Callers get all-or-nothing updates they can safely apply to shared documents. The cost is a deep copy
+of the document per call. Rejecting root removal differs from libraries that return `null`; it can be
+relaxed later if a caller needs it.
